Z||000000||Spyro86SE||11-26-2000||03:26 PM||||i was just wondering if anyone ever changed a starter in there fiero 2.8L mine went out on me and i'm going to change it today but if its not the starter what else could it be i got juice but it wont crank over it does'nt make any noise's like it wants to crank over <P>PLEASE HELP <BR>THANKS <BR>MIKEY <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/spryologo_1_1.gif"> ||||reg||9
Z||000001||StuGood||11-26-2000||05:39 PM||||I did.  One day I pulled my car out of the garage, and shut the motor off.  Got out, shut garage, got back into car, starter wouldn't operate.  At all.  No click, no nothing.  No warning.<P>Just get a rebuilt /remanufactured starter, and put it in.  It's not very hard.  In my case, I swiped the starter from another engine I had on the stand, until I got a reman. starter from parts store on the way home that day.<P>Disconnect battery cable at battery.  Jack up car, unbolt wires from starter.  Get heat shield out of your way.  Take out the 2 bolts that hold the starter in (15mm socket, I think), pull starter, save the starter shim.  Reuse same shim when you install new starter.  Reinstall wires, heat shield, etc., the way you found them.  Piece of cake.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||maninblack||11-26-2000||06:06 PM||||frm what i seen before,if it is a starter problem,it will make some "click click click"<BR>noise.Maybe its safe to check out your ignition module first||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||11-27-2000||02:27 AM||||Ignition module shouldn't affect cranking - I assume you meant you're not getting any cranking.<P>I started out describing how to R&R the starter, since you said you were about to do that.  Your original post also wanted to know what the problem might be, if it isn't the starter. I'll offer the following.<P>First do the obvious stuff like clean the battery terminals and check to make sure the positive cable is tight at the starter (do this only with the battery disconnected of course!).  Also inspect the negative cable's connection to the block. <P>If you suspect it's something other than the starter itself, hook up a remote starter button and try that.  If the starter works with the remote switch, then you know starter is okay (duh  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> ), and you need to look for trouble in the car wiring to the starter.  Clutch safety switch, connections and ignition switch would be prime suspects, I think.      <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 11-27-2000).]||||reg||1
